00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.270
from Marvel's Daredevil.

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:41.970
We've been on a bit of a Marvel
tear here on this channel.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:45.160
But before I leave the Marvel
legal cinematic universe,

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.090
at least for a little
while, I wanted to make sure

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:50.380
that I cover this highly
requested scene from Daredevil

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:54.130
where Frank Castle is put on
trial for all of the murders

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:56.920
that he has committed,
and he is being defended,

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.060
spoiler alert, by Matt Murdock

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.740
who is also Daredevil on the side.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.950
A lawyer's gotta have
a side hustle, I guess.

00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:06.710
As always, be sure to leave
your objections in the comments

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:08.530
which I'll either sustain or overrule.

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.040
And stuck around until
the end of the video

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:13.250
where I give the trial of
Frank Castle from Daredevil

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.310
a grade for legal realism.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:16.520
So without further ado,

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:20.259
let's dig into the trial of
Frank Castle on Daredevil.

00:01:20.259 --> 00:01:23.259
(handcuffs tapping)

00:01:28.664 --> 00:01:31.664
(suspenseful music)

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:37.630
All right, this is kind
of an interesting scene

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:41.210
that you don't see all that
often in legal procedurals.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:44.900
It takes a long time to get
a defendant out of the lockup

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.380
to be ready for a court appearance.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:50.900
And often, if court appearances
are at eight or nine a.m.,

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:53.510
then you might have to wake
up at three or four o'clock

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:54.800
in the morning and be transported

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.690
from the jail over to the courthouse

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:59.870
where your hearing is going to take place.

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:02.340
So we actually saw this
in the Paul Manafort trial

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:05.750
not too long ago where he waived his right

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.420
to appear in person so that
he wouldn't have to go through

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:11.770
all the rigmarole of getting
woken up, being transported,

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:14.630
which can take hours from
the jail to the courthouse,

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:18.170
and only to have some sort of
procedural hearing take place.

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:20.320
And that's why often,
you'll see defendants

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.000
waive their right to appear in court

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.520
and you just let the
lawyers take care of it,

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:26.750
unless it's really important
that the defendant be there,

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:29.770
like the trial is starting.

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:31.600
- If I get my hands on
the worthless intern

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.880
who organized these files--

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:36.790
- I'm sure that intern's just
following the DA's orders.

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:38.439
It's not officially sabotage.

00:02:38.439 --> 00:02:39.880
It just sets it back.

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:41.151
- Well, it's a cheap trick.

00:02:41.151 --> 00:02:43.410
- (sighs) We've got bigger problems,

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.300
like locking a defense.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:46.520
M'Naghten Rule is still our best bet.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:47.943
- What's that?

00:02:47.943 --> 00:02:50.030
- The insanity plea.
- Insanity plea.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:51.110
- Oh, whoa, guys.

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:53.280
- We need to buy a not guilty verdict.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.870
A high school debate team
could prove that Castle did it.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:57.360
- Okay, a couple of problems here.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.050
Number one is that an insanity plea

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:02.010
is very, very unlikely to work.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:03.460
The other problem is that New York

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:05.870
actually does not follow
the M'Naghten Rule.

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:09.400
New York follows a modified
version of the Model Penal Code.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:12.000
The Model Penal Code
is a theoretical rubric

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.100
for potential criminal
laws that was created

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.170
by a bunch of American scholars who saw

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:19.840
that there were some issues
with some of the antiquated laws

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:22.140
that the United States had at the time.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:24.090
And so they put together what they thought

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:26.500
would be a Model Penal
Code for the country.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:29.850
And New York adopted a version of the text

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:32.210
that's laid out by the Model Penal Code.

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:35.250
The New York law requires
that a defendant prove

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:36.790
by a preponderance of the evidence,

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:38.520
because it's an affirmative defense,

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:41.620
that at the time that the
defendant committed a crime

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:44.770
that he or she lacked substantial
capacity to know either,

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:47.800
one, the nature and
consequences of such conduct,

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:51.660
or two, that such conduct
was actually wrong.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:53.960
The defendant can prove either prong

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:57.160
and meet the requirements
of the insanity defense,

00:03:57.160 --> 00:03:59.220
but they have to prove that

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.080
by a preponderance of the evidence.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:05.180
So it's up to the defendant to
prove their insanity defense.

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:06.390
Now preponderance of the evidence

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:09.340
is generally the standard
that we use in civil law.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:10.970
It just means more likely than not.

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:14.370
So if you had both sides on a scale,

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:17.420
the side that had slightly
more weight than the other one,

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:20.050
that is a preponderance of the evidence.

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:21.440
So it can be 51%.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:23.120
That's enough to tip the
scales in your favor.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:25.680
If you are the defendant
in a criminal case,

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:27.800
you want the prosecution
to have all of the burden,

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.380
and they have to prove all
the elements of their case

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:31.450
beyond a reasonable doubt,

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:33.580
which is a much, much higher standard

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:35.260
than preponderance of the evidence.

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:36.093
So that's the second issue

00:04:36.093 --> 00:04:38.450
with Matt and Foggy
deciding on whether to make

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:40.460
a M'Naghten defense in this case.

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:42.490
It doesn't work in New York.

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:43.323
- Okay.

00:04:43.323 --> 00:04:45.535
And either way, we need an
opening statement tomorrow.

00:04:45.535 --> 00:04:47.940
- I can open with PTSD
as a mitigating factor,

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:49.360
focus on Castle's time at war.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:51.760
- So you think the war is
what made Frank what he is.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:54.580
- Our only goal is to reduce
the charges and set precedent.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:56.010
Castle made a lot of enemies, all right?

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:58.460
If he goes into general
population, he's as good as dead.

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:00.100
We need to get him somewhere
safe where he can get help,

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.488
and citing PTSD could do that.

00:05:02.488 --> 00:05:05.150
- I mean, none of us know
what Castle saw over there,

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:08.190
but research suggests that PTSD

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:10.660
can be triggered by new stressors.

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:11.560
- Like losing your family.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:13.520
- I need to get to work
on the opening statement,

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:15.607
but we need to be sure Castle
will get on board with this.

00:05:15.607 --> 00:05:17.520
- (sighs) I'll try.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:20.700
- So have they not spoken
to their client before?

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:25.140
Pro tip, if trial is tomorrow,
you really, really, really

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:26.960
need to talk to your client beforehand

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:29.290
to know what your
defenses are going to be.

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:30.800
- [Bailiff] All rise.

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:32.170
Court is now in session.

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:34.530
- All right, looks like a
pretty reasonable facsimile

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:35.800
of a New York courthouse.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:38.473
- [Bailiff] Indictment number 19864447,

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:42.333
the People versus Frank Castle.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:44.300
- [Judge] Be seated.

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:49.880
- Looks like you're a man down.-

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:52.230
- Ladies and gentlemen of the jury,

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:55.580
the defendant has been indicted
for several serious crimes.

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:57.960
But I'm instructing
you, as a point of law,

00:05:57.960 --> 00:05:58.977
that an indictment--

00:05:58.977 --> 00:06:00.363
- You know those college stress dreams

00:06:00.363 --> 00:06:02.366
when you show up to your
class in your underwear?

00:06:02.366 --> 00:06:03.199
- Yeah.

00:06:03.199 --> 00:06:04.032
- That's me right now.

00:06:04.032 --> 00:06:05.320
- [Judge] Presumed innocent
until proven guilty.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:08.380
- Goddamn, Matt was supposed
to deliver opening, not me.

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:09.830
- [Judge] Miss Reyes, are the People ready

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:11.850
to begin opening statements?

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:14.100
- I like that the judge
has already started

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:15.580
instructing the jury here

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:19.750
because so much of what
happens in an actual trial

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:23.430
is really opaque to members of the jury.

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:27.040
And so the judge always is
trying to do his or her best

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:29.260
to hold the hand of the jury,

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:31.080
to make sure that they
understand what's going on

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:33.700
and they don't prejudge
anyone just because

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:37.820
they happened to be, you know,
accused of some crime here.

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:39.770
Now, it's odd that Frank Castle

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:41.360
is still in his orange jumpsuit,

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:43.950
and he is shackled to the table.

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:46.317
If you were worried that
he was prone to violence

00:06:46.317 --> 00:06:49.120
and was a threat to someone,

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:53.570
that would be a reason to
actually keep him in chains.

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:55.480
But even under those circumstances,

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:58.170
you would still probably
let him wear a suit to court

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:01.240
so that he can at least
look semi respectable

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:04.710
and not like an actual
criminal at that point.

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:06.460
- Ladies and gentlemen of the jury,

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:11.653
Frank Castle brutally tortured
and murdered 30 people,

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:15.150
30 that we know of.

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:18.550
- Oh, man, it's very argumentative
for an opening statement.

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:23.500
- Acted as judge, jury and
the most violent executioner.

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:25.030
- She's way too close to the jury,

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:27.280
did not ask for permission
to enter the well.

00:07:29.208 --> 00:07:30.041
- And you will hear

00:07:30.041 --> 00:07:33.840
that the defendant's
victims were criminals.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:36.820
But the victims are not
on trial here today,

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:38.300
and justice does not belong

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:40.390
in the hands of a man like Frank Castle.

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:42.810
This isn't the wild West.

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:44.250
Justice is served here

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:49.330
in a court of law, and
it is up to each of you

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:53.510
to take back the city
from lawless vigilantes.

00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:55.570
- Ah, yeah.

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:58.270
This is a pretty improper
opening statement.

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:01.790
An opening statement is your
opportunity to give the jury

00:08:01.790 --> 00:08:05.380
a preview of what your
case is going to show.

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:08.050
It's your opportunity
to establish a theme.

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:10.500
But mostly, it's not for argument.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:13.070
It is for an opening statement

00:08:14.023 --> 00:08:16.780
to show what you intend to prove

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:19.320
and to set the guidelines.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:20.450
But at the same time, you're not allowed

00:08:20.450 --> 00:08:22.410
to overly personalize the jury

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:24.980
and make the jury overly emotional,

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:27.360
especially in a capital
murder case like this.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:28.890
The defense should
really be objecting here

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:31.220
because it's pretty improper.

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:32.660
- [Judge] Mr. Nelson, are you prepared

00:08:32.660 --> 00:08:34.880
to make your opening statement?

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:36.150
- She's good.

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:38.270
- No, no, she's not.

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:39.153
- Mr. Nelson?

00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:43.530
Mr. Nelson, are you reserving the right

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:45.270
to make your statement at a later time?

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:46.540
- Ooh, that's super cool.

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:47.373
Yeah, okay.

00:08:47.373 --> 00:08:48.800
So what the judge asked there

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:52.180
is a really interesting
piece of trial procedure

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:54.400
that actually does
exist in the real world.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:56.100
The defense has the option

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:58.360
to reserve their opening statement

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:00.430
for the start of their case in chief.

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:03.640
And if at the end of
the prosecution's case,

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:07.120
they have not at least shown
beyond a reasonable doubt

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:09.780
that the person being accused is guilty,

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:11.140
then they have effectively failed

00:09:11.140 --> 00:09:13.310
and you can move from what's
called a directed verdict

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:16.580
or a JNOV, which stops
the trial right there

00:09:16.580 --> 00:09:19.140
because the prosecution
didn't meet their burden.

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:22.820
Now, you are allowed to
reserve your opening statement

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:23.760
so that you can say that,

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:26.110
okay, well, if the prosecution
has met their burden

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:28.590
in the first instance, now
we're going to do our best

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:30.820
to take the ball from
our side of the court

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:32.720
and throw it back to the prosecution.

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:36.770
And at that point, you can
lay out what your entire case

00:09:36.770 --> 00:09:39.270
is going to be in your
own opening statement.

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:41.150
There are strategic reasons
why you would do this.

00:09:41.150 --> 00:09:44.020
Number one, if you don't have
a particularly good case,

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:49.020
you might reserve your opening
statement to a later time.

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:50.770
If you feel like the prosecution

00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:53.100
is really not going to be
able to meet their burden,

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:55.090
you might reserve your statement for later

00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:58.000
to put all of the focus
on the prosecution's case.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.410
And given that the lead trial
counsel hasn't shown up,

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:04.100
reserving your opening statement

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:06.070
is probably a good way to go here.

00:10:06.070 --> 00:10:06.903
- So,

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:11.850
you're 19, standing in hot sand,

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:13.283
sun burning down,

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:16.403
noise,

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:18.853
yelling, gunfire.

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:23.050
The only thing that you know for sure

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:25.810
is that you're surrounded by
an enemy that wants you dead.

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:27.860
- I don't Know that this is
necessarily argumentative.

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:30.270
I mean, he's sort of talking about facts.

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:32.090
And if you had prefaced this by saying

00:10:32.090 --> 00:10:34.580
that the evidence will
show this, this and this,

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:35.810
you could probably get away with it.

00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:38.100
The problem is that in a case like this,

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:39.470
the last thing that you would wanna do

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:40.880
is bring in character evidence.

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:43.770
I don't think that, number
one, that's relevant.

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:47.300
And number two, when you do
that, it opens the floodgate.

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:49.860
And man, the prosecution
would just bring in

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:52.980
witness after witness showing
Frank Castle's bad character.

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:56.130
So I think there are some
big strategic problems

00:10:56.130 --> 00:10:58.580
with Foggy's opening statement here.

00:10:58.580 --> 00:10:59.910
- But you do it.

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:01.330
You endure it.

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:02.950
Why?

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:04.150
Because you have orders.

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:06.020
And you have a duty.

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:08.460
- What does that have to do with anything?

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:10.083
- Your life doesn't end here.

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:14.403
You have people you love waiting at home.

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:20.120
Because aside from being
a decorated marine,

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:23.290
the man before you is a good husband

00:11:23.290 --> 00:11:24.690
and an excellent father.

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:26.500
- Okay, so he's going hard

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:28.040
into the good character right now.

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:30.420
- Returned from the hell of war

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:33.660
wanting nothing more
than to pick up his life.

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:34.623
But his wife,

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:38.290
young son and daughter

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:40.800
were brutally murdered by criminals,

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:43.540
and no one, not the police

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:46.410
and (scoffs) certainly
not the District Attorney,

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:47.893
stepped up to make it right.

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:50.690
See, Frank Castle never came home.

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:53.743
He just traded in one
war zone for another.

00:11:58.030 --> 00:11:58.863
This trial--

00:11:58.863 --> 00:12:01.350
- Ah, Matt Murdock shows up way too late.

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:03.650
- It's about the failure
of the justice system

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:08.030
and how one man, Frank Castle,
is being used as a pawn

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:09.840
to cover up that system's mistakes.

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:11.080
- Huh.

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:13.490
So that's not a great theme.

00:12:13.490 --> 00:12:14.323
I mean, it's okay.

00:12:14.323 --> 00:12:16.810
You wanna focus on the government's burden

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:18.790
to prove their case
beyond reasonable doubt.

00:12:18.790 --> 00:12:20.920
But Foggy hasn't given the jury

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:22.390
something to hang their hat on.

00:12:22.390 --> 00:12:24.730
If you can rely on good character
and that's all you have,

00:12:24.730 --> 00:12:25.563
then that's all you have.

00:12:25.563 --> 00:12:28.530
So you go after Frank's good character

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:31.130
and you say that that's a
mitigating circumstance,

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:32.640
but yeah, I don't think

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:34.326
that's a very good opening statement.

00:12:34.326 --> 00:12:35.159
- Where the hell have you been?

00:12:35.159 --> 00:12:36.090
- Look, I made a mistake, Karen.

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:36.960
I feel terrible about it.

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:38.410
But Foggy, I caught some
of your opening statement.

00:12:38.410 --> 00:12:39.250
You did great.

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:40.083
- It was amazing.

00:12:40.083 --> 00:12:42.260
- Hey, hey, thanks for
keeping us afloat, buddy.

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:43.093
- Okay.

00:12:43.093 --> 00:12:47.630
So if you are lead trial counsel
in a capital murder case,

00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:50.530
I think missing the opening statement

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:51.700
that you were supposed to give

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:54.300
is probably grounds for malpractice.

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:56.750
I wonder if Daredevil, Matt Murdock

00:12:56.750 --> 00:12:59.990
is canonically a good
lawyer in the comics.

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:00.950
You know what?

00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:02.620
Let me get an expert on this.

00:13:02.620 --> 00:13:03.453
Hey, Matt.

00:13:03.453 --> 00:13:04.480
Can you help out a sec?

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:05.313
- Thanks, Devon.

00:13:05.313 --> 00:13:08.100
As a longtime Daredevil
fan and fellow Matt myself,

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:09.940
I feel uniquely qualified to say

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:11.690
that Daredevil should be one of,

00:13:11.690 --> 00:13:14.710
if not the greatest fictional
lawyers of all time,

00:13:14.710 --> 00:13:15.880
He's very intelligent.

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:17.650
He graduated from Columbia Law School.

00:13:17.650 --> 00:13:19.610
He's also an amazing detective.

00:13:19.610 --> 00:13:22.880
And most notably, the accident
that blinded Matt as a child

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:26.210
gave him heightened senses,
one of which is super hearing

00:13:26.210 --> 00:13:28.990
which he's able to use to
his advantage during trials.

00:13:28.990 --> 00:13:31.520
Matt can hear a heartbeat
from across a room,

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:34.270
which he uses kind of
like a human lie detector.

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:37.160
He can tell if a juror is on his side

00:13:37.160 --> 00:13:40.260
and potentially change his
argument to win more support.

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:43.450
He can also tell if a witness
is lying or telling the truth.

00:13:43.450 --> 00:13:45.170
He's essentially psychic.

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:48.710
However, the biggest obstacle
between Matt and legal success

00:13:48.710 --> 00:13:50.540
is frequently Matt himself.

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:53.350
He has a strong sense of
Catholic guilt that often

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:56.910
forces him to take on lost
causes that he can't win.

00:13:56.910 --> 00:14:00.360
Also, Matt's double life
as Daredevil forces him

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:03.790
to split his time between
being a lawyer and a vigilante,

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:06.920
which in some cases has
forced him to lose clients

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:08.710
and even lose cases.

00:14:08.710 --> 00:14:10.700
In conclusion, based off the comics,

00:14:10.700 --> 00:14:12.930
when you take into
account his intelligence

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:16.030
and his super hearing, Matt
Murdock should be one of,

00:14:16.030 --> 00:14:19.380
if not the best fictional
lawyers of all time.

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:20.213
- Thanks, Nando.

00:14:20.213 --> 00:14:22.620
I guess now I know never
to hire Matt Murdock.

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:24.980
- Dr. Gregory Tepper,
how long have you been

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:27.200
medical examiner for the city of New York?

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:29.250
- 14 years, give or take.

00:14:29.250 --> 00:14:30.390
- And how many death certificates

00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:32.570
would you estimate you've
signed in that time?

00:14:32.570 --> 00:14:33.403
- Huh?

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:36.660
Um, I don't, uh--

00:14:36.660 --> 00:14:38.500
- It's probably in the tens of thousands.

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:40.947
- Objection, Your Honor,
leading the witness.

00:14:40.947 --> 00:14:42.870
- (laughs) Oh, my god.

00:14:42.870 --> 00:14:44.870
That is such a stupid objection.

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:48.050
Cross examination is supposed
to be all leading questions.

00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:49.410
And on top of that, this would be

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:52.130
a really, really softball
question for a medical examiner.

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:55.410
A medical examiner testifies a lot.

00:14:55.410 --> 00:14:57.990
I mean, part of their job is to testify

00:14:57.990 --> 00:15:00.910
in these kinds of criminal cases,

00:15:00.910 --> 00:15:02.550
so they're gonna have a lot of experience,

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:04.790
especially someone who has
been the medical examiner

00:15:04.790 --> 00:15:07.900
of the city of New York for 14 years.

00:15:07.900 --> 00:15:09.860
So he would have a ballpark figure

00:15:09.860 --> 00:15:12.350
for the number of death
certificates that he signed a lot

00:15:12.350 --> 00:15:14.180
because that's a question
that's going to come up

00:15:14.180 --> 00:15:16.250
in a cross examination like this

00:15:16.250 --> 00:15:18.800
where you're allowed to lead the witness.

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:21.030
- I have something I need to say.

00:15:21.030 --> 00:15:21.863
- Doctor?

00:15:21.863 --> 00:15:25.583
- Your Honor, I'm sorry, but
I have to, on the record.

00:15:26.610 --> 00:15:28.660
I need to say something about what I did.

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:32.085
- Clear the gallery.

00:15:32.085 --> 00:15:33.600
- Yes, Your Honor.

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:37.373
All jury members, please exit
back to the deliberation room.

00:15:38.510 --> 00:15:39.480
Everyone in the gallery,

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:41.944
please make your way out of
the courtroom at this time.

00:15:41.944 --> 00:15:42.777
- What?

00:15:42.777 --> 00:15:44.280
Why are they taking Frank Castle out?

00:15:44.280 --> 00:15:45.810
He's the defendant.

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:48.450
He definitely gets to stay at this point.

00:15:48.450 --> 00:15:50.140
- Your Honor, I believe the defense

00:15:50.140 --> 00:15:52.420
intends to distract the
jury and make this case

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:55.380
about some impossible-to-prove
conspiracy theories.

00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:56.213
- Impossible to prove?

00:15:56.213 --> 00:15:58.860
Your ME just confirmed that he
doctored the autopsy records.

00:15:58.860 --> 00:16:01.010
- Yeah, Murdock is totally right there.

00:16:01.010 --> 00:16:02.190
- If my office finds out that your firm

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:03.360
had anything to do with this--

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:04.193
- Our firm?

00:16:04.193 --> 00:16:06.110
For all we know, it was
you who sent those goons.

00:16:06.110 --> 00:16:07.400
- I've heard enough.

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:10.210
It's clear to me that
whatever Dr. Tepper may know

00:16:10.210 --> 00:16:12.550
about these autopsy
reports has been tainted

00:16:12.550 --> 00:16:16.310
by threats made by person
or persons unknown.

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:17.730
- Then the defense asks for a mistrial.

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:19.040
- Not on your life, Mr. Nelson.

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:20.040
- What?

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:20.873
- I'm still not convinced these documents,

00:16:20.873 --> 00:16:23.770
whatever they may be, are
even relevant to this trial.

00:16:23.770 --> 00:16:26.850
I'm striking the doctor's entire testimony

00:16:26.850 --> 00:16:28.620
and instructing the jurors to disregard

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:30.580
anything they may have heard.

00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:32.960
- Okay, a couple of different
things going on here.

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:35.070
The judge is probably right

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:38.210
that the medical examiner's testimony

00:16:38.210 --> 00:16:40.840
about Frank's family is not relevant.

00:16:40.840 --> 00:16:42.970
That being said, in this particular trial,

00:16:42.970 --> 00:16:45.710
this is the medical examiner
that not only examined

00:16:45.710 --> 00:16:48.160
Frank's family and conducted the autopsies

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:51.490
on Frank's family, but he
also conducted the autopsies

00:16:51.490 --> 00:16:52.760
on all of the victims.

00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:56.100
The medical examiner has been
testifying for probably days.

00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:58.730
I mean, I know they're shooting
it as a matter of hours,

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:03.730
but this kind of testimony
over the deaths of 17 people

00:17:04.210 --> 00:17:06.910
plus Frank's family,
that's gonna take days,

00:17:06.910 --> 00:17:09.120
and he's gonna be a very,
very important witness

00:17:09.120 --> 00:17:10.110
for the state.

00:17:10.110 --> 00:17:14.570
It's incredibly unfair, I
would say clearly prejudicial,

00:17:14.570 --> 00:17:17.560
for the state to be able to show the jury

00:17:17.560 --> 00:17:19.430
the testimony of this medical examiner

00:17:19.430 --> 00:17:21.450
and then prevent the defense

00:17:21.450 --> 00:17:24.010
from being able to cross
examine the medical examiner.

00:17:24.010 --> 00:17:28.050
You are constitutionally
allowed to face your accusers.

00:17:28.050 --> 00:17:29.540
And it's crazy for the judge

00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:30.900
not to declare a mistrial right now.

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:32.440
The jury has been tainted.

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:34.170
You can't unring that bell,

00:17:34.170 --> 00:17:36.270
and there's no way to go forward.

00:17:36.270 --> 00:17:38.490
I think if you were the
prosecution, you'd be pissed too

00:17:38.490 --> 00:17:41.220
because without the medical
examiner's testimony,

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:42.940
I'm not sure how you prove up your case.

00:17:42.940 --> 00:17:45.220
- I wonder if you could tell us

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:48.100
how Lieutenant Frank
Castle won the Navy Cross?

00:17:48.100 --> 00:17:49.560
- Due to the nature of that mission,

00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:50.907
you'll have to understand

00:17:50.907 --> 00:17:53.403
that the precise
circumstances are classified.

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:02.080
- How about the part that's not?

00:18:03.982 --> 00:18:06.440
- Lieutenant Frank Castle
was part of a small team.

00:18:06.440 --> 00:18:08.580
He was conducting a close
target reconnaissance

00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:10.310
in the vicinity of the Hindu Kush.

00:18:10.310 --> 00:18:12.820
The mission became compromised,

00:18:12.820 --> 00:18:15.003
taking enemy contact on three sides.

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:19.290
Lieutenant Castle wanted to abort,

00:18:19.290 --> 00:18:20.780
said the mission was a bust,

00:18:20.780 --> 00:18:23.280
pulling the plug would save lives.

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:25.370
Officer in charge said no.

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:27.690
- I feel like this is
that scene out of Airplane

00:18:27.690 --> 00:18:30.460
where the wife is asking Striker

00:18:30.460 --> 00:18:32.830
about what he's going to do.

00:18:32.830 --> 00:18:34.020
And he says--

00:18:34.020 --> 00:18:35.980
- My orders came through.

00:18:35.980 --> 00:18:37.430
My squadron ships out tomorrow.

00:18:37.430 --> 00:18:41.600
We're bombing the storage depots
at Daiquiri at 1800 hours.

00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:43.860
We're coming in from the
north, below their radar.

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:44.937
- And she says--

00:18:45.810 --> 00:18:46.883
- When will you be back?

00:18:46.883 --> 00:18:49.150
- I can't tell you that.

00:18:49.150 --> 00:18:50.130
It's classified.

00:18:50.130 --> 00:18:52.090
- Precise circumstances are classified.

00:18:52.090 --> 00:18:55.330
- It really seems like this
character has just talked about

00:18:55.330 --> 00:18:57.240
a whole bunch of classified information

00:18:57.240 --> 00:19:00.200
and there can't possibly be
any classified information left

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:03.040
that he hasn't already
divulged to the jury.

00:19:03.040 --> 00:19:05.020
- My father served in Vietnam.

00:19:05.020 --> 00:19:07.030
Do you know what he told me about medals?

00:19:07.030 --> 00:19:07.930
- No, ma'am.

00:19:07.930 --> 00:19:11.310
- He said the only people
who truly know what happened

00:19:11.310 --> 00:19:12.623
are the ones that were there.

00:19:13.862 --> 00:19:15.940
You told a nice story, Colonel,

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:20.210
but how can we know that it
happened the way you described?

00:19:20.210 --> 00:19:22.500
- Well, perhaps I wasn't clear.

00:19:22.500 --> 00:19:23.463
I was there, ma'am.

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:26.830
That officer that didn't listen to Frank,

00:19:26.830 --> 00:19:29.930
got his men trapped,
you're looking at him.

00:19:29.930 --> 00:19:31.960
- This is a great dramatic scene,

00:19:31.960 --> 00:19:33.460
I mean, regardless of legal accuracy.

00:19:33.460 --> 00:19:35.010
I just, I love the turnaround here.

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:37.280
This is a great example
of the legal truism

00:19:37.280 --> 00:19:39.580
that you do not ask question
on cross examination

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:42.290
that you don't already know the answer to.

00:19:42.290 --> 00:19:46.390
- Are you aware that Frank
Castle's wife, son, and daughter

00:19:46.390 --> 00:19:48.100
were all murdered right in front of him

00:19:48.100 --> 00:19:50.430
when he sustained the
brain injury in question,

00:19:50.430 --> 00:19:52.960
an injury which you say keeps
him in a perpetual state

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:55.740
of mental and emotional chaos?

00:19:55.740 --> 00:19:56.900
- I am, yes.

00:19:56.900 --> 00:19:58.080
- With that in mind, would you say

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:02.300
that Frank Castle's mental
state satisfies the definition

00:20:02.300 --> 00:20:04.220
of extreme emotional disturbance?

00:20:04.220 --> 00:20:06.550
- Objection, calls for a conclusion.

00:20:06.550 --> 00:20:09.100
- Your Honor, Dr. Lee is
an expert on the brain.

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:11.040
He is qualified to an opinion,

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:13.250
and said opinion is not only relevant

00:20:13.250 --> 00:20:15.370
but imperative to the case.

00:20:15.370 --> 00:20:18.739
- Okay, so this is a really
interesting objection here.

00:20:18.739 --> 00:20:20.660
I think they're a little
confused on the terminology

00:20:20.660 --> 00:20:23.570
but this kind of objection makes sense.

00:20:23.570 --> 00:20:25.670
So you are allowed to call experts,

00:20:25.670 --> 00:20:28.910
and experts are allowed to opine
in a way that lay witnesses

00:20:28.910 --> 00:20:31.300
are not allowed to
opine when they testify.

00:20:31.300 --> 00:20:33.050
That's really the whole
point of an expert.

00:20:33.050 --> 00:20:35.410
That being said, generally speaking,

00:20:35.410 --> 00:20:38.040
witnesses are not allowed to opine

00:20:38.040 --> 00:20:41.090
as to what we call the
ultimate issue in the case.

00:20:41.090 --> 00:20:42.807
So you can't ask a witness,

00:20:42.807 --> 00:20:44.360
"Do you think the defendant is guilty?"

00:20:44.360 --> 00:20:47.550
Well, guilt is one of the
ultimate issues in a case.

00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:50.300
And if you are arguing
insanity, for example,

00:20:50.300 --> 00:20:53.867
you can't say, "Does that
mean that the defendant

00:20:53.867 --> 00:20:58.867
"is actually insane," for the
purposes of the insanity plea,

00:20:59.220 --> 00:21:02.040
because that's the ultimate
issue, and only the jury

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:04.620
is able to decide the ultimate issues.

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:06.240
If emotional disturbance

00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:08.850
is an ultimate issue
for the trier of fact,

00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:12.160
then it would be improper
for the expert to opine.

00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:16.930
But if emotional disturbance
is a neurological term

00:21:16.930 --> 00:21:20.980
that is sort of a stepping
stone for whatever legal issue

00:21:20.980 --> 00:21:23.170
that they are trying to
prove, then it would be okay

00:21:23.170 --> 00:21:25.790
for this neurologist
to make that testimony.

00:21:25.790 --> 00:21:27.560
- Mr. Castle, you've been charged

00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:30.460
with multiple capital
crimes, been called a killer

00:21:30.460 --> 00:21:34.000
incapable of empathy or remorse.

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:35.600
- Hmm.

00:21:35.600 --> 00:21:37.250
Yeah,

00:21:37.250 --> 00:21:38.083
so I hear.

00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:40.660
- Frank,

00:21:40.660 --> 00:21:41.710
may I call you Frank?

00:21:43.950 --> 00:21:44.900
- Yeah.

00:21:44.900 --> 00:21:45.733
- That's good.

00:21:45.733 --> 00:21:47.060
You wanna humanize your defendant.

00:21:47.060 --> 00:21:49.690
- Frank, we've heard a
lot about neurochemistry

00:21:50.740 --> 00:21:52.840
and psychology, and all things unfolding,

00:21:52.840 --> 00:21:55.240
scientifically and
otherwise, inside your brain,

00:21:56.320 --> 00:21:59.000
but I just have one
question I want to ask.

00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:00.530
- I hate it when people
say they have one question

00:22:00.530 --> 00:22:02.030
because it's never just one question.

00:22:02.030 --> 00:22:03.460
- What happened that day,

00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:05.710
the day your family was
so tragically killed.

00:22:07.240 --> 00:22:08.090
It's okay, Frank.

00:22:09.550 --> 00:22:11.111
I understand it's difficult.

00:22:11.111 --> 00:22:12.510
- Do you?

00:22:12.510 --> 00:22:13.423
Do you understand?

00:22:14.687 --> 00:22:16.923
'Cause I don't think you understand shit.

00:22:22.073 --> 00:22:22.906
- I'd like permission

00:22:22.906 --> 00:22:24.320
to treat the witness
as hostile, Your Honor?

00:22:24.320 --> 00:22:26.520
- Okay, so this is actually a good example

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:30.900
of when you can actually
treat a witness as hostile.

00:22:30.900 --> 00:22:33.820
It's a request that's made
in legal dramas all the time,

00:22:33.820 --> 00:22:34.730
but they usually get it wrong.

00:22:34.730 --> 00:22:36.220
Here, they basically get it right.

00:22:36.220 --> 00:22:39.300
All it means to treat a witness as hostile

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:42.360
is to be able to ask
them leading questions.

00:22:42.360 --> 00:22:45.460
Yes, it is a witness
that you have provided,

00:22:45.460 --> 00:22:47.600
but they're being obstreperous in a way

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:49.010
that they are not answering

00:22:49.010 --> 00:22:50.910
open-ended, non-leading questions.

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:53.130
So you ask permission from the judge

00:22:53.130 --> 00:22:54.700
to ask leading questions

00:22:54.700 --> 00:22:56.950
that would normally not be
allowed on direct examination.

00:22:56.950 --> 00:23:00.920
Now, that being said,
it's always, always risky

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:03.690
to put a criminal defendant on the stand.

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:06.450
When your witness stops complying

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:09.070
with your softball questions,

00:23:09.070 --> 00:23:10.610
you know you're in the danger zone

00:23:10.610 --> 00:23:13.220
and you really just
have to cut your losses.

00:23:13.220 --> 00:23:15.430
- I'm gonna tell you exactly
what kind of man you are.

00:23:15.430 --> 00:23:16.800
- No, you're not allowed to do that.

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:18.860
You still have to ask questions.

00:23:18.860 --> 00:23:21.490
- You're the kind of man this city needs,

00:23:21.490 --> 00:23:22.780
because, ladies and gentlemen of the jury,

00:23:22.780 --> 00:23:26.270
we all know this city
needs help, needs it now.

00:23:26.270 --> 00:23:28.660
Not tomorrow, not next week,
not when the day comes,

00:23:28.660 --> 00:23:31.200
when the corruption that
Wilson Fisk left in his wake--

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:33.973
- Yeah, objection, argument.

00:23:36.340 --> 00:23:37.890
- 'Cause this city's been sick.

00:23:39.150 --> 00:23:40.990
And the cops, they can't fix it alone.

00:23:40.990 --> 00:23:44.070
They need, we all need men and women--

00:23:44.070 --> 00:23:46.140
- This is totally improper, 100% improper.

00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:47.840
- Who risk their lives

00:23:47.840 --> 00:23:50.480
so that we can walk safe at
night in our own neighborhoods,

00:23:50.480 --> 00:23:52.270
the ones our esteemed
District Attorney here

00:23:52.270 --> 00:23:53.970
is trying so hard to destroy.

00:23:53.970 --> 00:23:56.080
New York needs these people.

00:23:56.080 --> 00:23:57.582
We need--

00:23:57.582 --> 00:23:59.230
- It's hard for me to even watch this

00:23:59.230 --> 00:24:00.872
because it's so cringe inducing.

00:24:00.872 --> 00:24:01.705
(crowd applauds)

00:24:01.705 --> 00:24:02.538
- [Judge] Order!

00:24:02.538 --> 00:24:04.230
- Okay, and now we're gonna applaud

00:24:04.230 --> 00:24:05.700
the speech that's being made

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:07.860
in the middle of a direct examination.

00:24:07.860 --> 00:24:09.370
The judge would not allow this.

00:24:09.370 --> 00:24:10.940
The prosecution would be objecting.

00:24:10.940 --> 00:24:13.883
- [Frank] Your Honor, can I say something?

00:24:15.470 --> 00:24:17.181
- [Judge] You may.

00:24:17.181 --> 00:24:18.231
- You know those, uh,

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:25.053
those people, the ones I put
down, the people I killed?

00:24:27.300 --> 00:24:29.223
I want you to know that
I'd do it all again.

00:24:29.223 --> 00:24:30.060
(crowd gasps)

00:24:30.060 --> 00:24:31.270
This is a circus, all right?

00:24:31.270 --> 00:24:32.103
It's a charade.

00:24:32.103 --> 00:24:33.060
It's an act.

00:24:33.060 --> 00:24:34.280
This (beep) about

00:24:34.280 --> 00:24:35.113
how crazy I am.
- Language.

00:24:35.113 --> 00:24:36.480
- I ain't crazy.

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:37.510
- And that, ladies and gentlemen,

00:24:37.510 --> 00:24:39.820
is why you don't put a
defendant on the stand

00:24:39.820 --> 00:24:42.220
unless you absolutely have to.

00:24:42.220 --> 00:24:43.323
Bad idea.

00:24:43.323 --> 00:24:46.156
(whimsical music)

00:24:47.090 --> 00:24:50.280
Okay, that was the trial of Frank Castle

00:24:50.280 --> 00:24:52.541
in Marvel's Daredevil.

00:24:52.541 --> 00:24:54.340
(gavel bangs)

00:24:54.340 --> 00:24:56.170
A lot of good stuff, a lot of bad stuff,

00:24:56.170 --> 00:24:57.840
definitely interesting to watch

00:24:57.840 --> 00:24:59.270
since I haven't seen it in a while.

00:24:59.270 --> 00:25:00.200
On the plus side,

00:25:00.200 --> 00:25:01.720
